[Music]  hi I'm Toby and welcome to the joy of  mathematics I'm so glad you could join  me today I'm going to run some ideas  across the bottom of the screen and  these are ideas which we will come to  understand today it's another beautiful  day outside today although it is a  little windy I've gone ahead and already  drawn a tree for us but a tree is a  little bit like an iceberg in that what  you can see on the surface is only a  small part of the story what is going to  be really important to us today is what  we can see under ground a tree could not  exist without its roots now last time I  saw you we were talking about logarithms  and how they can be used to rewrite  exponents one particularly popular  exponent is the square a number times  itself we could have 3 times 3 is equal  to 9 4 times 4 which is 16 or even 5  times 5 which is 25 these are all  squares and could be written as 3  squared 4 squared or 5 squared but what  had some leaves carried by the wind with  to come across and land right here and  right here so that all we could see was  the result we knew that something  squared was equal to 16 but we couldn't  quite remember what well what we would  need to do would be to find the square  root of 16 roots are what allow a tree  to grow the better the roots the more  nutrients they can collect and the more  support they can provide just like in  life having support can make it feel  easier to achieve great things  although support can often be hard to  come by or difficult to recognize  just drawing in our roots here  you can see these roots would be very  good at providing some moral and  structural support to the tree we also  forgot to add the Apple to our tree  before so we can go ahead and sneak that  in now now in these square roots  there are 16 units of dead that's the  area of the square an area of a square  is calculated from its base times its  height and since these are the same if  we know the length of just one side we  know the square root we can go ahead and  divide this up into a grid and if the  length of each side was 3 then we would  have 9 units of debt but if instead it  was 5 on each side we would have 25  units of debt but the sweet spot for us  and you're going to see it here is that  if each link of the roots each side of  the square is 4 units long and in total  we will have 16 units of dirt sitting  right here in our roots and that's  exactly what we wanted so we know that  the square root of 16 will be equal to 4  all these numbers that I've mentioned 9  16 25 they're all called perfect squares  because they are the squares of whole  numbers we could look at a non-perfect  square say 20 although all numbers are  perfect in their own way and we could  look well 20 is between 16 and 25 so the  square root of 20 would be somewhere  between 4 and 5 and in fact it is 4  point 4 7 now there's actually another  solution to all of these square roots  and I hate to mention it as an hour  I thought because nobody likes being an  afterthought but we could have also had  for example - four times - for being  equal to 16 because two wrongs make a  right but in our case having a negative  side length wouldn't make a lot of sense  so we were fine  to just take the positive solution there  is actually a symbol that tells us to  look underground and find the square  root of a number and that is this heart  beat kind of symbol here called the  radical because it is pretty radically  cool now I remember when I very first  encountered the symbol on some math  homework at school but I was pretty  confused because I had never seen it  before I didn't know what it was called  and I didn't know how to find out I  wanted to google it but I didn't know  how to describe it in the Google search  do use type in tick like symbol or  heartbeat I kind of liked the name  heartbeat symbol now if you like me you  might be a little bit curious as to the  root of this square root symbol and  there seems to be a little bit of  disagreement about where exactly it  comes from one story that I've heard as  to its origin is that it comes from the  Arabic word for root and it comes from  the first letter of the word which looks  a little bit like this the Latin word  for root is actually radix and it comes  from radix quadratum which actually  means the side of a square some  mathematicians wrote this as capital R X  now that might have made its way to  becoming the modern symbol or it might  have also come from a lowercase R also  for ratings but there's one more  possible origin story and that is that  some mathematicians used to denote a  square root as putting a dot or a few  dots above the numbers that they wanted  to take the square  and it's thought that maybe somewhere  along the way these dots just acquired a  bit of an upstroke and that became our  symbol what we do know is that in 1637  Descartes was the first to use the  square root symbol with this line over  the top that has a name it's called the  van keulen and that includes everybody  in the square root who was supposed to  be there it's like a big group hug the  square root symbol typically means to  just take the principal or the positive  result of the square root so there is  only one leaf that would satisfy the  square root of 16 is equal to leaf only  one solution would satisfy that but  instance did we were to have leaf  squared is equal to 16 there are two  Leafs that would satisfy this and we  would have to write our answer as being  plus or minus 4 the square root can also  just as easily be written as 16 to the  half we can start using fractional  powers and for other roots say the cube  root we will just extend this to be 16  to the one-third or we could write 3 in  our little square root symbol and that  would give us a different solution  squaring any number positive or negative  will give us a positive result  squaring 0 will give us 0 so there is  actually no way for a negative number to  have any real root but we can make  things more inclusive if we invite over  our imaginary friends if we have we  introduce our friend I squared to be  equal to minus 1 then we would have that  I  is equal to the square root of -1  technically - I would also be equal to  the square root of -1 but by convention  we just take positive I to be our square  root minus 1 our principal root then if  we wanted to do the square root of minus  16 we could do that and it would give us  the square root of 16 times the square  root of -1 leading us to an answer of 4i  moral of the story is don't let anyone  tell you that you can't have a square  root you can even take the square root  of an imaginary number but you can see  my other video for how to do that so  that has been our trip underground to  understand square roots if you haven't  already then take a walk near some trees  and try to imagine what their hidden  roots look like I'd like to wish you  happy studying and I hope that you have  an absolutely mathematical day  [Music]  you

